Hooke's Law relates the applied force ($\Delta F$), the modulus of elasticity ($Y$), the change in length ($\Delta L$), and the original length ($L_0$):
$$\Delta F = Y \frac{\Delta L}{L_0}$$

We are given:
*   $\Delta F = F_B = 4.9 \times 10^5 \text{ dynes}$
*   $Y$ (Young's modulus) $= 7 \times 10^{11} \text{ dynes}\cdot\text{cm}^{-2}$
*   $|\Delta L|$ (change in length) $= 0.05 \text{ cm}$

We need to solve for the original length $L_0$:
$$L_0 = Y \frac{|\Delta L|}{\Delta F}$$

$$L_0 = (7 \times 10^{11} \text{ dynes}\cdot\text{cm}^{-2}) \times \frac{0.05 \text{ cm}}{4.9 \times 10^5 \text{ dynes}}$$

